\title{The Last Theorem:\\Countable Provability, Finite Computation, and Holographic Storage}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{July 22, 2026}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
A statement over a fixed finite alphabet is a finite string, and the provable statements of a deductive system form a subset of the set of such strings. This paper studies the collision between the resulting countable infinity and finite physical resources. For every productive deductive system---one with infinitely many distinct theorems---the theorem set is countably infinite and therefore admits a bijection with the natural numbers. Removing any finite set of discovered theorems leaves infinitely many undiscovered. For a budget of $N$ theorems, the maximal coverage of the first $n$ entries of an enumeration is $f_N(n)=\min(N,n)/n$; this quantity is nonnegative, bounded above by $N/n$, and converges to zero. We then analyze an idealized holographic memory. In Planck units, a Schwarzschild radius $r=aM$, horizon area $A=4\pi r^2$, and entropy $S=A/4$ imply $S(a,M)=\pi a^2M^2$. Thus capacity scales quadratically with mass, is strictly increasing for nonnegative mass when $a>0$, and eventually dominates every nonnegative linear capacity. Nevertheless, every fixed finite mass yields a finite theorem budget, so its discoverable fraction also converges to zero.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}

Mathematical knowledge is expressed through finite inscriptions: formulas, derivations, programs, and books. The supply of potential inscriptions is infinite, but any individual inscription is finite. This combinatorial fact permits all statements in a fixed language to be arranged in a sequence. If the provable statements are infinite in number, they form a countably infinite library.

Physical discovery has a different character. A computer performing finitely many operations can emit only finitely many distinct outputs. A civilization confined to a finite operation budget therefore occupies a finite region of an infinite mathematical library. Figures such as $10^{120}$ operations may motivate the discussion, but no particular cosmological estimate is needed here: finiteness alone drives the central result.

The analysis has three layers. First, finite syntax supplies countability. Second, a finite discovery set leaves an infinite complement and has vanishing coverage in a specified enumeration. Third, black-hole thermodynamics supplies a candidate improvement in absolute capacity: because event-horizon entropy is proportional to area and Schwarzschild radius is proportional to mass, storage scales as the square of mass. Quadratic growth outperforms linear growth, but a finite quadratic quantity remains finite.

The claims are conditional. We assume a productive deductive system rather than developing all of the syntax and derivation rules of ZFC. We model a physical cap as a finite theorem budget rather than deriving a numerical cap from cosmology. We use the Bekenstein--Hawking relation in Planck units and do not identify entropy with accessible memory without qualification. These choices isolate a mathematical core while identifying where richer models must add assumptions.

\section{Finite languages and theorem libraries}

\begin{definition}[Alphabet and statement]
For $b\in\mathbb N$, let
\[
\Sigma_b=\{0,1,\ldots,b-1\}.
\]
A \emph{statement} over $\Sigma_b$ is a finite string of symbols. The set of all statements is
\[
\Sigma_b^*=\bigcup_{\ell=0}^{\infty}\Sigma_b^\ell,
\]
where $\Sigma_b^\ell$ is the set of strings of length $\ell$ and $\Sigma_b^0$ contains the empty string.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Productive deductive system]
A deductive system $D$ over $\Sigma_b$ is represented by a set $T_D\subseteq\Sigma_b^*$, whose elements are its theorems. It is \emph{productive} when $T_D$ is infinite.
\end{definition}

Productivity is the exact hypothesis needed below. In a concrete treatment, $T_D$ consists of well-formed formulas possessing finite derivations from specified axioms and inference rules. Productivity should not be confused with consistency, although familiar consistent theories extending elementary arithmetic are productive.

\begin{lemma}[Countability of the ambient language]
For every finite alphabet $\Sigma_b$, the set $\Sigma_b^*$ is countable.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
For each length $\ell$, there are $b^\ell$ strings, hence finitely many. Order strings first by length and then lexicographically within each length. This enumerates every finite string. Equivalently, $\Sigma_b^*$ is a countable union of finite sets.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Countable Theorem Library]
If $D$ is a productive deductive system over a finite alphabet, then $T_D$ is countably infinite. In particular, there is a bijection
\[
e_D:T_D\longrightarrow\mathbb N.
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
The ambient set $\Sigma_b^*$ is countable, and every subset of a countable set is countable. Thus $T_D$ is countable. Productivity says it is infinite. Every infinite countable set is in bijection with $\mathbb N$.
\end{proof}

The inverse of $e_D$ gives an abstract enumeration $t_0,t_1,t_2,\ldots$ without repetition. This establishes an ordering, not a finite completion procedure. Every fixed theorem has a finite index, but no finite index contains the entire library. Bare countability also supplies neither an efficient proof search nor proof-length bounds.

When $b=0$, the empty string is the only statement. The language remains countable, but no productive theorem subset exists. This boundary case explains why nonempty syntax is necessary for an infinite theorem library.

\section{Finite discovery and vanishing coverage}

Let $F$ be the set of statements discovered, stored, or emitted during a physical history. If the history permits only finitely many relevant operations and each operation has finite output, then $F$ is finite. The next conclusion needs only that fact.

\begin{theorem}[Inexhaustibility under finite discovery]
Let $D$ be productive and let $F$ be any finite set of statements. Then $T_D\setminus F$ is infinite.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
If $T_D\setminus F$ were finite, then $T_D\cap F$ would also be finite because it is a subset of $F$. But
\[
T_D=(T_D\cap F)\cup(T_D\setminus F),
\]
so $T_D$ would be a union of two finite sets, contradicting productivity.
\end{proof}

This theorem is ordering-independent and permits $F$ to contain non-theorems. It says more than that one theorem is missed: every finite history leaves infinitely many provable statements outside its archive.

Now fix an enumeration of $T_D$. If a resource budget permits at most $N$ distinct theorems, then among an initial segment of $n$ entries it can cover at most $\min(N,n)$. For $n>0$, define
\[
f_N(n)=\frac{\min(N,n)}{n},
\]
and set $f_N(0)=0$ for convenience. This is an optimistic upper envelope: it grants that every available output is a distinct theorem positioned inside the prefix under examination.

\begin{lemma}[Basic bounds]
For all $N,n\in\mathbb N$, $f_N(n)\ge0$. For every positive $n$,
\[
f_N(n)\le \frac{N}{n}.
\]
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
Nonnegativity follows from nonnegative numerator and denominator, together with the convention at zero. The upper bound follows from $\min(N,n)\le N$ after division by positive $n$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Vanishing Discovery Fraction]
For every fixed finite budget $N$,
\[
\lim_{n\to\infty}f_N(n)=0.
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
The basic bounds give
\[
0\le f_N(n)\le\frac{N}{n}.
\]
For fixed $N$, the upper bound converges to zero. The squeeze theorem proves the claim. Equivalently, once $n>N$, one has $f_N(n)=N/n$.
\end{proof}

This is not a claim that a finite archive is unimportant. It is an asymptotic comparison between a bounded numerator and an unbounded prefix size. Natural density of an arbitrary infinite subset can change when a countable set is rearranged, but the present fixed-budget result survives every enumeration because no prefix can contain more than $N$ archived entries.

If the budget instead grows with $n$, becoming $N(n)$, the same envelope is $\min(N(n),n)/n$. It converges to zero whenever $N(n)=o(n)$, may approach a positive constant under linear growth, and may equal one if resources keep pace with the prefix. The fixed-budget theorem is the limiting case relevant to a completed finite physical history.

\section{Holographic storage}

A possible response to finite storage is to seek a medium with a better resource law. Black-hole thermodynamics motivates capacity associated with a boundary area rather than an ordinary volume. The following model isolates its algebraic scaling.

\begin{definition}[Schwarzschild area-law model]
Let $M\in\mathbb R$ be mass and let $a\in\mathbb R$ be a mass-to-radius coefficient. Define
\[
r(a,M)=aM,\qquad A(r)=4\pi r^2,
\]
and, in Planck units, define the Bekenstein--Hawking entropy by
\[
S(a,M)=\frac{A(r(a,M))}{4}.
\]
\end{definition}

The word ``storage'' refers here to the scaling of the number of distinguishable states. Conversion to bits and accessible memory requires additional constants and operational assumptions.

\begin{theorem}[Horizon Area-to-Mass Law]
For all real $a$ and $M$,
\[
S(a,M)=\pi a^2M^2.
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Substitution gives
\[
S(a,M)=\frac{4\pi(aM)^2}{4}=\pi a^2M^2.
\]
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[General quadratic scaling]
For every real scale factor $c$,
\[
S(a,cM)=c^2S(a,M).
\]
In particular, $S(a,2M)=4S(a,M)$, and $S(a,3)=9S(a,1)$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{proof}
Apply the area-to-mass law and $(cM)^2=c^2M^2$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Strict growth with nonnegative mass]
If $a>0$ and $0\le M_1<M_2$, then
\[
S(a,M_1)<S(a,M_2).
\]
Thus $M\mapsto S(a,M)$ is strictly increasing on $[0,\infty)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
The coefficient $\pi a^2$ is positive. Squaring is strictly increasing on nonnegative reals, so $M_1^2<M_2^2$. Multiplication by $\pi a^2$ preserves the inequality.
\end{proof}

The restriction to nonnegative masses is mathematically essential because the square function is not strictly increasing on all real numbers.

\begin{theorem}[Eventual domination of linear storage]
Let $k,c,M\in\mathbb R$ satisfy $k>0$, $c\ge0$, and
\[
M\ge\frac{c}{k}.
\]
Then
\[
cM\le kM^2.
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
The hypotheses imply $M\ge0$. Multiplying $M\ge c/k$ by $k>0$ yields $kM\ge c$. Multiplication by $M\ge0$ then yields $kM^2\ge cM$.
\end{proof}

Taking $k=\pi a^2$ for $a\ne0$ shows that horizon entropy eventually dominates every capacity proportional to mass. The condition $k>0$ cannot be removed: a zero quadratic coefficient cannot overtake a positive linear capacity.

\section{The holographic limit remains finite}

For fixed $a$ and $M$, define the idealized integer capacity
\[
N_{\mathrm{BH}}(a,M)=\max\left(0,\left\lfloor S(a,M)\right\rfloor\right).
\]
For physical parameters $a>0$ and $M\ge0$, this is simply $\lfloor\pi a^2M^2\rfloor$.

\begin{theorem}[Holographic Scarcity]
For every fixed finite pair $a,M\in\mathbb R$,
\[
\lim_{n\to\infty}
\frac{\min\bigl(N_{\mathrm{BH}}(a,M),n\bigr)}{n}=0.
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
For fixed finite inputs, $S(a,M)=\pi a^2M^2$ is a finite real number. Hence $N_{\mathrm{BH}}(a,M)$ is a fixed natural number. The Vanishing Discovery Fraction Theorem applies with $N=N_{\mathrm{BH}}(a,M)$.
\end{proof}

The result separates absolute and relative improvement. As mass varies, quadratic capacity eventually exceeds every linear capacity. Once a finite mass is fixed, however, the resulting capacity is constant while theorem prefixes continue to grow. The decisive divide is not linear versus quadratic growth, but finite versus infinite extent.

\section{Algorithms and numerical observables}

A canonical string enumerator loops through lengths $\ell=0,1,2,\ldots$ and emits the $b^\ell$ strings at each length in lexicographic order. Through length $L$, the number emitted is
\[
\sum_{\ell=0}^{L}b^\ell=
\begin{cases}
L+1,&b=1,\\[3pt]
\dfrac{b^{L+1}-1}{b-1},&b\ne1.
\end{cases}
\]
Running time is at least proportional to total output size, and retaining all strings takes exponential space in $L$ when $b>1$. A concrete theorem enumerator additionally needs a proof checker and a dovetailed traversal of candidate derivations.

The finite-budget profile evaluates $f_N(n)$ at selected prefix sizes. Computing $m$ values costs $O(m)$ arithmetic operations and $O(m)$ output space, or constant auxiliary space if values are streamed. Logarithmically spaced prefixes show the plateau $f_N(n)=1$ for $n\le N$ and reciprocal decay thereafter.

The holographic profile evaluates $S(a,M)=\pi a^2M^2$ for selected masses, requiring constant arithmetic work per mass. The ratio $S(a,cM)/S(a,M)$ is $c^2$ whenever the denominator is nonzero. The crossover between $kM^2$ and $cM$ is $M=c/k$ for $k>0$.

These numerical procedures demonstrate mathematical scaling rather than derive a physical bit count. In SI units, dimensions and constants must be restored, and thermodynamic entropy must be divided by $k_B\log 2$ to obtain bits.

\section{Interpretation, applications, and limitations}

The established chain is robust: finite strings are countable; an infinite theorem subset is countably infinite; deleting finitely many discoveries leaves an infinite remainder; fixed finite coverage vanishes against growing prefixes; the area law produces quadratic mass scaling; and every fixed finite mass still yields finite coverage.

A numerical cap such as $10^{120}$ operations depends on a physical model and measured parameters. Moreover, an operation is not automatically a theorem. Search can expend resources on malformed candidates, invalid derivations, and duplicate conclusions. Conversely, a compact axiom or algorithm may describe many consequences without explicitly storing them. The theorem-budget abstraction intentionally suppresses these distinctions.

Horizon entropy is likewise not a blueprint for readable memory. Construction energy, access latency, noise, causal structure, lifetime, evaporation, and error correction all matter. The area-law model establishes a scaling consequence and asks whether it crosses the finite--infinite boundary. For fixed finite mass, it does not.

Countability is also distinct from decidability. For effectively axiomatized theories, finite candidate proofs can be checked and valid proofs can be enumerated by dovetailing, while non-theorems may never receive certificates. Bare countability is weaker: it guarantees an ordering but not a feasible search. Thus ``all theorems can be listed'' means an unending process in which each theorem eventually occurs, not a finite moment at which the list is complete.

Finally, provability is not semantic truth. In sufficiently expressive consistent systems, some true sentences are independent of the axioms. The scarcity result already holds inside the provable subset: a finite archive misses infinitely many provable statements before independent truths are considered.

Applications include bounded automated reasoning, where finite-time systems emit only finite theorem sets; scientific archives, where finite databases cannot explicitly contain every consequence of an infinite deductive closure; complexity-aware theorem search based on proof or formula length; and information geometry, where boundary area rather than bulk volume controls capacity.

\section{Future research}

A fuller account should replace the abstract theorem set by first-order syntax, finite derivation trees, the ZFC axiom schemes, and an explicit derivability relation. Productivity could then be proved by constructing infinitely many syntactically distinct theorems, such as iterated conjunctions of a fixed equality axiom.

Countability should next be strengthened to effective enumeration. A dovetailed search can inspect all finite candidate derivations, with a completeness proof showing that every valid finite derivation is eventually checked. Such a model distinguishes candidates inspected, proofs accepted, and distinct theorems emitted.

Complexity-sensitive scarcity calls for counting theorems with proofs of length at most $n$. Finite alphabets give upper bounds, while useful lower bounds require explicit proof families. Formula length, proof length, prefix-free description complexity, and upper Banach density also provide more intrinsic alternatives to an arbitrary ordering.

The physical model should restore $G$, $c$, $\hbar$, and $k_B$, perform dimensional analysis, and convert entropy to bits. Competing operation limits---including Margolus--Levitin, Bekenstein, and de Sitter bounds---should be presented as explicit physical hypotheses. Holographic capacity should be coupled to lifetime, energy, latency, and evaporation.

Finally, semantic truth should be introduced so that provable statements, true statements, independent statements, and emitted discoveries become separate sets. This would connect finite discovery horizons to incompleteness without conflating failure to find a sentence before a deadline with unprovability.

\section{Conclusion}

A productive deductive system over a finite alphabet has a theorem library in bijection with the natural numbers. Every finite discovery set leaves infinitely many theorems behind, and a fixed budget $N$ covers only $\min(N,n)/n$ of a prefix of length $n$, a fraction converging to zero.

Black-hole thermodynamics changes the scale of the finite budget. Under the Schwarzschild and area-law assumptions, entropy is $\pi a^2M^2$: doubling mass quadruples capacity, nonnegative mass increases capacity strictly, and quadratic storage eventually dominates every nonnegative linear law. Fixed finite mass nevertheless gives fixed finite capacity, so the limiting theorem share remains zero.

The central boundary is therefore not ordinary versus exotic computation, nor linear versus quadratic growth. It is the boundary between every finite physical archive and a countably infinite mathematical library. A final theorem printed at the end of computation would mark the end of discovery, not the end of what remains to be discovered.

